-- Passage rg9-theme-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3cde1c9f-2260-5e69-a747-46caedf46519', 'rg9-theme-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Seven O'Clock$q$, $q$Daniel Santos spent most of October building an app for his grandmother. Lola Remedios took four medicines a day, and twice that month she had worried aloud that she might have missed the afternoon one. Daniel, who had just finished his first coding course, saw a problem with a clean solution.

The app was simple, and he was proud of it. Each pill had a color and a time. At the right moment the phone chimed and showed a giant picture of the pill with a single green button that said TAKEN. He tested it forty times. He installed it on her phone himself and showed her how it worked, twice.

"Very smart," Lola said, patting his hand. "You are so smart."

For the next two weeks the app recorded nothing. Every evening at seven, though, Daniel's phone rang. "Anak," Lola would say, "did I take the blue one today?" He would ask whether she had checked the app, and she would say she could not find her phone, even though she was calling him from it.

Daniel made the text bigger. He added a voice that read the pill names out loud. He made the chime louder. Nothing changed, except that Lola started asking about his classes during the seven o'clock call, and then about his friends, and the calls grew longer.

"She isn't confused," his mother said one night, after he had complained for ten minutes. "She remembers her pills. She wants a reason to call you."

Daniel opened his code that weekend. He did not delete anything. He changed one line, so that at seven each evening the app showed a new message in the same giant letters: CALL DANIEL. Below it was a single green button.

Lola pressed it every night.$q$, 'draft')
