-- Passage rg9-theme-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c6ed9f45-fcff-52c9-83c0-ef74c1de5407', 'rg9-theme-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Route 9$q$, $q$Farah Haddad had ridden the Route 9 bus to school since September, and she had a single complaint about it: Mr. Ibarra, the driver, was slow.

He was not slow on the road. He was slow at the stops. He waited for Mr. Okoro, who walked with a cane, to settle into the front seat before pulling away. He kept a cardboard box of lost gloves under his seat and held it up whenever someone looked cold. He knew most riders by name, and he said each name out loud, every morning, as if he were taking attendance. Farah, who liked to arrive early to finish her homework, timed the delays. They added up to six minutes a day.

In March Mr. Ibarra took a week off, and a substitute drove Route 9. She was polite and very efficient. She did not say anyone's name. She pulled away the moment the doors closed, and on Tuesday she pulled away while Mr. Okoro was still crossing the sidewalk. He raised his cane, and the bus did not stop. Farah watched him grow smaller in the back window.

The bus arrived early every day that week, and Farah finished all her homework. She also noticed that the bus had gone quiet. People stared at their phones. Nobody passed the gloves. A girl who usually sat with Farah moved to the back without saying hello.

On Monday Mr. Ibarra was back. "Farah," he said as she climbed on, the way he always had, and she was surprised by how glad she was to hear it.

At the next stop she saw Mr. Okoro on the sidewalk. She stood up before the bus had fully stopped, waited at the door until he reached the step, and helped him to the front seat. Mr. Ibarra watched in the mirror and said nothing. He did not need to. The bus was late again that morning, and no one seemed to mind.$q$, 'draft')
